  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nRain is gradually ending from northwest to southeast early this\nmorning as the main trough responsible for our extended period \nof rain exits eastward. Precip is now confined to primarily east\nof I-57, with the remainder of the forecast area expected to be\nprecip-free by sunrise. Clouds will clear this morning as weak \nridging and plenty of dry air aloft advect in from the west, \nleading to generally sunny skies by the afternoon.\n\nSurface high pressure will then drift eastward across Wisconsin\nand Lower Michigan this afternoon into Saturday morning. After \na cool (but seasonable) day with highs in the mid 60s today, \ndecoupling winds within a notably drier airmass will promote \nplenty of cooling tonight. Low temperatures in the lower to mid \n40s will be common away from the core of the Chicago heat \nisland. Would not be surprised to see some readings in the upper\n30s for the typically colder spots across the northwest half of\nthe CWA. Will also need to keep an eye out for patchy shallow \nfog toward sunrise around the Illinois/Kankakee River valleys \nnorthward into the south and west Chicago metro where heavier \nrain fell the past couple days and slightly higher lake-modified\nair advects inland.\n\nWith the upper-jet displaced well to the north and persistent \nhigh pressure cutting off Gulf moisture through next week, we \nwill head into a rather quiet stretch of weather. Cannot rule \nout a few isolated showers with a cold front brushing the area \nto the north on Sunday and again on Wednesday, but the area may \nvery well end up precip-free through much of next week. Temps \nwill be fairly seasonable during this period. Monday will likely\nbe the coldest night of the stretch with outlying areas \npossibly falling into the upper 30s.\n\nKluber",
